$ felis --help
felis: a terminal whose sessions outlive their windows.

Opens or attaches a window, or drives the daemon headlessly via `sessions` and `notifications`; `felis <name> …` runs `felis-<name>`.

Usage: felis [OPTIONS] [-- <CMD>...] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  sessions       List, drive, and inspect sessions without opening a window
  notifications  Stream desktop notifications from sessions
  config         Inspect the config file felis reads without dialing a daemon
  doctor         Check every layer felis needs and diagnose failures
  version        Compare this build against the GUI client and running daemon
  daemon         Inspect the daemon behind sessions
  attach         Open a window attached to an existing session
  window         Act on the window this command runs in
  ssh            Re-point this window at the daemon on another host, over SSH
  bridge         Speak JSON lines on stdin/stdout for a non-Rust client
  completions    Print a shell completion script to stdout
  help           Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [CMD]...
          Run this program in the new window instead of the default `$SHELL` (`xterm -e`).
          
          Arguments after `--` are taken literally as argv. Ignored with a subcommand.

Options:
  -V, --version
          Print this binary's own build and exit.
          
          Self-report only: no subprocess, no daemon, no network, so it refuses the globals rather than ignore them; `felis version` compares all three builds.

      --host <user@host>
          Connect to a remote daemon over SSH (`ssh <host> felis-daemon relay`), for window launches and headless verbs alike. Only `sessions spawn` and window launches start a cold daemon there; the read and drive verbs exit 2 instead, `version` and `doctor` report it as not running, and completion never dials

      --ssh-arg <TOKEN>
          Extra token passed verbatim to `ssh` before the destination (repeatable).
          
          felis passes tokens directly without interpretation; only `ssh` parses them. Requires `--host`.

      --socket <PATH>
          Use this daemon socket instead of the default per-user path.
          
          Allows multiple daemons to coexist under one user. Mutually exclusive with `--host`.

      --config <PATH>
          Read this `config.toml` instead of the platform default.
          
          The selected file must exist. Applies to window launches, `config`, and `doctor`; other verbs refuse it.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions --help
List, drive, and inspect sessions without opening a window.

Every verb talks to the daemon directly and exits; `--format json`/`jsonl` is the stable scripting surface. See `felis sessions help <verb>`.

Usage: felis sessions <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list     List sessions, one line each. `--format json` emits one object holding the whole roster
  info     Print one session's details: id, size, idle age, title, cwd, tags
  send     Type text into a session, window attached or not
  kill     Terminate a session and remove it from the daemon
  evict    Evict every window attached to a session; the session lives on
  capture  Print a session's screen as plain text
  spawn    Create a detached session and print its id
  switch   Point one window attached to a session at another session
  search   Search a session's scrollback and screen
  tag      Add and remove labels, for pickers and scripts to filter on
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions list --help
List sessions, one line each. `--format json` emits one object holding the whole roster

Usage: felis sessions list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

      --tag <TAG>
          Show only sessions carrying at least one of the given tags (repeatable; the match is "any of"). Tags are set with `sessions tag` or `spawn --tag`

  -h, --help
          Print help

$ felis sessions info --help
Print one session's details: id, size, idle age, title, cwd, tags

Usage: felis sessions info [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix; optional `0x` strip

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help

$ felis sessions send --help
Type text into a session, window attached or not.

Pasted into the session (bracketed paste if requested); `--raw` sends bytes untouched. CLI input lands alongside GUI keystrokes.

Usage: felis sessions send [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX> [TEXT]

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

  [TEXT]
          Literal text to send. `-` reads from stdin

Options:
      --raw
          Send the bytes untouched, without bracketed-paste quoting. Use when injecting control bytes or escape sequences that must not be quoted as paste data

      --wait
          Block until the next command completes and print its exit code: decimal on stdout, `-` for bare `OSC 133 ; D`.
          
          Subscription starts before input injection. With no text or keys, watches an existing command. Needs OSC 133 shell integration.

      --timeout <SECS>
          With `--wait`: give up after this many seconds (exit 1). Without it, wait indefinitely.
          
          `0` is a deadline already past: the wait gives up at once.

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

      --key <CHORD>
          Press a named key after the text: `--key up`, `--key enter`, `--key ctrl+c` (repeatable, pressed in order).
          
          Keys are encoded against current keyboard modes; hand-written `--raw` escapes send the wrong bytes to mode-switched programs.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions kill --help
Terminate a session and remove it from the daemon.

`--format json` emits `{"id":"<hex>"}` with the resolved full id; an unknown or ambiguous prefix is a typed error object on stderr with exit 1, never a result.

Usage: felis sessions kill [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions evict --help
Evict every window attached to a session; the session lives on.

Returns to the detached pool. Exits 0 whether a window was attached or not, 1 for no such session. Named `evict` because `detach` is the one-window key action.

Usage: felis sessions evict [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions capture --help
Print a session's screen as plain text.

Soft-wrapped rows are stitched back into one logical line. `--format jsonl` emits one unstitched object per row (`text`, `row`, `soft_wrap_continued`) followed by the stream terminal.

Usage: felis sessions capture [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `jsonl`: one JSON object per line on stdout, ending in exactly one terminal object
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, jsonl]

      --ansi
          Reproduce each cell's color and style as inline escapes

      --source <SOURCE>
          Region to capture: `visible` (default), `scrollback`, `command-output` / `last-command` (the last OSC 133 command's ranges; their `--format jsonl` rows count from 0 within the region)
          
          [default: visible]
          [possible values: visible, scrollback, command-output, last-command]

      --lines <N>
          Print only the last N rows of the selected source (the tail an agent polling a session wants). A filter, not a renumbering: `--format jsonl` row indices keep untrimmed capture values

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions spawn --help
Create a detached session and print its id.

The one sessions verb that starts a daemon when none is running, on the host it dials, `--host` included. Bare `spawn` runs the shell a new window would; `-- <cmd> [args...]` runs another.

Usage: felis sessions spawn [OPTIONS] [-- <CMD>...]

Arguments:
  [CMD]...
          Program + args. Empty: use the daemon's default shell

Options:
      --cwd <PATH>
          Set working directory for the child

      --env <KEY=VAL>
          Environment overrides: `KEY=VAL`. May be repeated. Applied last, over the daemon's inherited-then-sanitized env and its identity stamps; `FELIS_SESSION_ID` is rejected

      --rows <N>
          Initial geometry, rows. Must be given with `--cols`; neither means the daemon's default grid

      --cols <N>
          Initial geometry, cols. Must be given with `--rows`

      --tag <TAG>
          Label the session at creation (repeatable). These are the same labels `sessions tag` adds, but already set when the session first becomes listable, so a `list --tag` filter can never catch it untagged

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions switch --help
Point one window attached to a session at another session.

The by-id counterpart of the in-window switch keybinding. Moves one window (defaults to this one under `$FELIS_SESSION_ID`).

Usage: felis sessions switch [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Session to switch to: hex id or any unique prefix

Options:
      --from <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Session whose attached window should move. Defaults to `$FELIS_SESSION_ID` (the session this command runs in)

      --attachment <ID>
          Move this attachment specifically, instead of the session's last window input owner. Ids come from `sessions list` / `sessions info` with `--format json`; an id that has since detached is an error, never a redirect to another window

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions search --help
Search a session's scrollback and screen.

Matches stream grep-style as `<line>:<text>` (line -1 is newest scrollback row). Substring match by default, `--regex` for regex.

Usage: felis sessions search [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX> <PATTERN>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

  <PATTERN>
          Pattern to look for. Substring by default; with `--regex` the daemon's `regex` crate parses it

Options:
      --regex
          Treat the pattern as a regex

      --case-insensitive
          Case-insensitive match

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `jsonl`: one JSON object per line on stdout, ending in exactly one terminal object
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, jsonl]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis sessions tag --help
Add and remove labels, for pickers and scripts to filter on.

Positional TAGs are added; `--remove <TAG>` removes. Both may appear in one call for atomic relabeling. Re-adding an existing tag or removing an absent one is a no-op.

Usage: felis sessions tag [OPTIONS] <ID-OR-PREFIX> [TAG]...

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

  [TAG]...
          Labels to add

Options:
      --remove <TAG>
          Label to remove (repeatable)

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis notifications --help
Stream desktop notifications from sessions.

Relays OSC 9 / 99 / 777 notifications; felis draws no popups itself. Pipe into an external notifier (`notify-send`, `terminal-notifier`).

Usage: felis notifications <COMMAND>

Commands:
  subscribe  Stream desktop notifications (OSC 9 / 99 / 777) until interrupted
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis notifications subscribe --help
Stream desktop notifications (OSC 9 / 99 / 777) until interrupted.

Streams every session's notifications by default. felis draws no popup; pipe output into an external notifier (`notify-send`, `terminal-notifier`).

Usage: felis notifications subscribe [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --session <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Only relay notifications from this session (hex id or any unique prefix)

      --once
          Print the first (matching) notification and exit 0: the notification-side `sessions send --wait`, for TUI programs that emit no OSC 133 marks. Exits 1 if the stream ends first

      --timeout <SECS>
          Give up after this many seconds without a (matching) notification and exit 1. Requires `--once`: an endless subscribe has no timeout.
          
          `0` is a deadline already past: the wait gives up at once.

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `jsonl`: one JSON object per line on stdout, ending in exactly one terminal object
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, jsonl]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis config --help
Inspect the config file felis reads without dialing a daemon.

`path` prints the file location, `check` validates diagnostics, and `show-effective` prints the merged configuration.

Usage: felis config <COMMAND>

Commands:
  path            Print where felis looks for `config.toml`
  check           Validate the config file and print every problem it has
  show-effective  Print the configuration felis would actually use: defaults filled in and the selected client's overlay folded in
  help            Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis config path --help
Print where felis looks for `config.toml`.

The path is reported whether or not the file exists: a first-time user has none, and "where would it go" is the question they are asking.

Usage: felis config path [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis config check --help
Validate the config file and print every problem it has.

Reports all errors and warnings rather than stopping at the first. Exits 0 when clean or warnings only, 1 when any error is present. A missing default file exits 0; a missing `--config` file is an error.

Usage: felis config check [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --client <ID>
          Which `[client.<id>]` overlay to fold in. Defaults to the GUI client id.
          
          Other `[client.*]` sections are untouched and not checked because their schema may differ.
          
          [default: felis]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis config show-effective --help
Print the configuration felis would actually use: defaults filled in and the selected client's overlay folded in.

The human framing is TOML, the format you wrote it in, so what you read is what you would edit.

Usage: felis config show-effective [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --client <ID>
          Which `[client.<id>]` overlay to fold in. Defaults to the GUI client id.
          
          Other `[client.*]` sections are untouched and not checked because their schema may differ.
          
          [default: felis]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis doctor --help
Check every layer felis needs and diagnose failures.

Inspects daemon, config, terminfo, GPU, clipboard, and SSH. Exits 1 on failures; unreachable daemons are findings, not exit 2. `doctor report` adds the environment for a bug report.

Usage: felis doctor [OPTIONS]
       felis doctor <COMMAND>

Commands:
  report  Print the checklist and this machine's environment as Markdown to paste into a bug report
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis doctor report --help
Print the checklist and this machine's environment as Markdown to paste into a bug report.

Run it from the affected window and review it before posting; exits 0 whenever the report is written, failing checks included.

Usage: felis doctor report [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

