$ felis daemon --help
Inspect the daemon behind sessions.

Reports build, wire version, and resource usage against ceilings. Never starts a daemon.

Usage: felis daemon <COMMAND>

Commands:
  status   Report the running daemon: build, wire version, and accounted resources
  stop     Stop the running daemon over IPC
  upgrade  Replace the running daemon's binary, keeping every session
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis daemon status --help
Report the running daemon: build, wire version, and accounted resources.

Surfaces observed usage against admission limits. Never starts a daemon: exits 2 if none is running.

Usage: felis daemon status [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis daemon stop --help
Stop the running daemon over IPC.

Refuses while sessions remain, reporting how many; `--force` destroys them and `--when-empty` waits them out. Never starts a daemon.

Usage: felis daemon stop [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --force
          Destroy every session, then stop

      --when-empty
          Refuse new sessions and stop once the last one ends

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis daemon upgrade --help
Replace the running daemon's binary, keeping every session.

The daemon execs the `felis-daemon` installed beside this `felis`. A refusal leaves it serving as before; the fallback is `felis daemon stop --when-empty`. Never starts a daemon.

Usage: felis daemon upgrade [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis attach --help
Open a window attached to an existing session.

Bare `felis` is the create-new counterpart. The globals reach the window and belong before the verb (`felis --config work.toml attach <id>`); see `felis --help`.

Usage: felis attach <ID-OR-PREFIX>

Arguments:
  <ID-OR-PREFIX>
          Hex id or any unique prefix

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis window --help
Act on the window this command runs in.

The object is the window, not the session behind it: `retarget` re-points it at another daemon. Run from inside a felis window; outside one there is nothing to act on.

Usage: felis window <COMMAND>

Commands:
  retarget  Re-point this window at a daemon on this machine
  help      Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis window retarget --help
Re-point this window at a daemon on this machine.

Bare, it re-dials the default local daemon: the way back from `felis ssh`. A SOCKET path names a second local daemon.

Usage: felis window retarget [OPTIONS] [SOCKET] [-- <CMD>...]

Arguments:
  [SOCKET]
          Socket of the local daemon to re-dial. Absent: the default local daemon

  [CMD]...
          Program + args for the fresh target session, in the trailing slot the front door spells the same way. Refused with `--session`, which attaches to what is already running

Options:
      --attachment <ID>
          Move this attachment specifically, instead of the session's last window input owner. Ids come from `sessions list` / `sessions info` with `--format json`; an id that has since detached is an error, never a redirect to another window

      --session <TARGET-PREFIX>
          Attach to this session on the *target* daemon (hex id or unique prefix, resolved after dialing) instead of creating a fresh one. Mutually exclusive with a `-- <cmd>` override

      --from <PREFIX>
          Move a different window: the session whose window should re-dial (hex id or prefix). Defaults to `$FELIS_SESSION_ID` (the window this command runs in)

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis ssh --help
Re-point this window at the daemon on another host, over SSH.

The window re-dials the destination and lands there; closing it leaves the remote session running. `felis --host <dest>` is the other verb: it opens a new window on that host instead.

Usage: felis ssh [OPTIONS] <user@host> [-- <CMD>...]

Arguments:
  <user@host>
          Any destination `ssh` accepts: `user@host`, an `~/.ssh/config` alias, or an `ssh://user@host:port` URI, passed verbatim

  [CMD]...
          Program + args for the fresh target session, in the trailing slot the front door spells the same way. Refused with `--session`, which attaches to what is already running

Options:
      --ssh-arg <TOKEN>
          Extra token passed verbatim to `ssh` before the destination (repeatable): `--ssh-arg=-p --ssh-arg=2222`. Handy for an ad-hoc VM not worth an `~/.ssh/config` entry; felis never interprets the tokens

      --attachment <ID>
          Move this attachment specifically, instead of the session's last window input owner. Ids come from `sessions list` / `sessions info` with `--format json`; an id that has since detached is an error, never a redirect to another window

      --session <TARGET-PREFIX>
          Attach to this session on the *target* daemon (hex id or unique prefix, resolved after dialing) instead of creating a fresh one. Mutually exclusive with a `-- <cmd>` override

      --from <PREFIX>
          Move a different window: the session whose window should re-dial (hex id or prefix). Defaults to `$FELIS_SESSION_ID` (the window this command runs in)

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output framing: `human` (default) or `json`: one object on stdout, with a failure as one error object on stderr
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis bridge --help
Speak JSON lines on stdin/stdout for a non-Rust client.

Persistent stdio bridge relaying JSONL requests and replies to the daemon. Stderr carries log lines only, never protocol. Exits if daemon is unreachable.

Usage: felis bridge

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ felis completions --help
Print a shell completion script to stdout.

Supports bash, elvish, fish, powershell, and zsh. The fish and zsh scripts also complete session IDs dynamically against running daemons.

Usage: felis completions <SHELL>

Arguments:
  <SHELL>
          Target shell
          
          [possible values: bash, elvish, fish, powershell, zsh]

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
